\chapter{Rates, exposure and offsets}

\section{The problem}

Suppose two clinics report accident numbers. Clinic A saw 40 accidents, Clinic B
saw 20. Which is more dangerous?

You cannot say --- not until you know Clinic A serves 100{,}000 people and Clinic
B serves 10{,}000. Then A's rate is 0.4 per thousand and B's is 2.0 per thousand,
and B is five times worse.

This is the single most common serious mistake in applied count regression:
modelling the raw count when the observations had different amounts of
\emph{exposure} --- different follow-up times, different populations at risk,
different areas, different numbers of trials.

\begin{plainenglish}[Offset in one sentence]
An offset is a predictor whose coefficient you refuse to estimate because you
already know it must be exactly 1, which is what turns a model for counts into a
model for rates.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{The mathematics, which is short}

You want to model the rate $\mu_i / t_i$, where $t_i$ is the exposure:
\[
\log\!\left(\frac{\mu_i}{t_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i.
\]
Split the log:
\[
\log(\mu_i) - \log(t_i) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i
\]
\[
\log(\mu_i) = \log(t_i) + \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i.
\]

That $\log(t_i)$ sits in the linear predictor like a covariate --- but with its
coefficient nailed to 1. That is precisely what \fn{offset()} does.

\begin{keyidea}
\fn{offset(log(exposure))}. Always the \emph{log} of the exposure, because the
link is a log. Putting the raw exposure in an offset is a real and surprisingly
common bug.
\end{keyidea}

\section{A simulated example where it matters}

<<offset-sim>>=
set.seed(42)
n       <- 400
region  <- factor(sample(c("urban", "rural"), n, replace = TRUE))
## Person-years of follow-up differ a lot -- and urban areas are bigger
pyears  <- round(exp(rnorm(n, mean = ifelse(region == "urban", 8.0, 6.5), sd = 0.6)))
## TRUE rate is 30% HIGHER in rural areas
true_rate <- ifelse(region == "rural", 0.0013, 0.0010)
events  <- rpois(n, lambda = true_rate * pyears)
dat     <- data.frame(events, pyears, region)
aggregate(cbind(events, pyears) ~ region, data = dat, FUN = sum)
@

The truth we built in: the rural rate is 1.3 times the urban rate. Now fit the
model two ways.

<<offset-fits>>=
## WRONG: ignores that urban areas were watched for far longer
m_wrong <- glm(events ~ region, family = poisson, data = dat)

## RIGHT: models the rate per person-year
m_right <- glm(events ~ region + offset(log(pyears)), family = poisson, data = dat)

round(cbind(
  wrong_IRR = exp(coef(m_wrong)),
  right_IRR = exp(coef(m_right))
), 3)
@

The model without the offset reports a rural rate ratio of
\Sexpr{round(exp(coef(m_wrong))["regionrural"], 2)} --- it says rural areas are
\emph{safer}, and would be reported as strongly significant. The model with the
offset recovers \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(m_right))["regionrural"], 2)}, close to the
true 1.3. The first answer is not merely imprecise; it points the wrong way.

<<offset-plot, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.8, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: raw counts make rural areas look safer, because they were observed for less time. Right: once expressed as rates per person-year, the true pattern appears.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
boxplot(events ~ region, data = dat, col = c(adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.5),
        adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.5)), border = c(PAL["primary"], PAL["accent"]),
        ylab = "raw event count", main = "Raw counts (misleading)")
grid_book()
boxplot(1000 * events / pyears ~ region, data = dat,
        col = c(adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.5), adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.5)),
        border = c(PAL["primary"], PAL["accent"]),
        ylab = "events per 1000 person-years", main = "Rates (correct comparison)")
grid_book()
@

\section{The alternative syntax}

\R{} accepts the offset in the formula or as an argument. They are equivalent:

<<offset-syntax>>=
a <- glm(events ~ region + offset(log(pyears)), family = poisson, data = dat)
b <- glm(events ~ region, offset = log(pyears), family = poisson, data = dat)
all.equal(coef(a), coef(b))
@

\begin{rulebox}[Prefer the in-formula version]
Writing \fn{offset(log(pyears))} inside the formula means \fn{predict()} on new
data automatically knows it needs a \fn{pyears} column. With the separate
\fn{offset =} argument you must remember to supply it yourself at prediction time,
and forgetting is silent.
\end{rulebox}

\section{Predicting from a model with an offset}

Two different questions, two different answers.

<<offset-predict>>=
## Q1: expected COUNT for a rural area observed for 2000 person-years
predict(m_right, newdata = data.frame(region = "rural", pyears = 2000),
        type = "response")

## Q2: the underlying RATE per person-year -- set exposure to 1
predict(m_right, newdata = data.frame(region = c("urban", "rural"), pyears = 1),
        type = "response")

## ... which is easier to read per 1000 person-years
1000 * predict(m_right, newdata = data.frame(region = c("urban", "rural"), pyears = 1),
               type = "response")
@

Setting exposure to 1 makes $\log(t) = 0$, which removes the offset from the linear
predictor and leaves the pure rate.

\section{When exposure is not time}

Exposure means ``how much opportunity did this unit have for the event?''. It takes
many forms:

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.34\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.32\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Outcome} & \textbf{Exposure} & \textbf{Offset term} \\
\midrule
Hospital admissions per patient  & follow-up time     & \fn{offset(log(years))} \\
Crimes per district              & population size    & \fn{offset(log(pop))} \\
Defects per manufactured batch   & batch size         & \fn{offset(log(n\_items))} \\
Species counts per plot          & plot area          & \fn{offset(log(area))} \\
Insurance claims per policy      & policy-years       & \fn{offset(log(exposure))} \\
Website conversions per campaign & impressions served & \fn{offset(log(impressions))} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Should the coefficient really be 1?}

An offset assumes events scale exactly proportionally with exposure: double the
population, double the accidents. That is usually right, but you can test it.
Put the log-exposure in as an ordinary covariate and see whether its estimated
coefficient is near 1:

<<offset-test>>=
m_free <- glm(events ~ region + log(pyears), family = poisson, data = dat)
round(coef(summary(m_free))["log(pyears)", ], 4)

## Formal test: is the offset model (coefficient fixed at 1) adequate?
anova(m_right, m_free, test = "LRT")
@

The estimated coefficient is close to 1 and the likelihood-ratio test gives a large
$p$-value, so fixing it at 1 is fine here.

\begin{plainenglish}[If the coefficient is clearly not 1]
Then the relationship is not proportional --- perhaps larger regions have
economies of scale, or a saturating process. Leave \fn{log(exposure)} in as a free
covariate and say so. You are no longer modelling a simple rate, and you should
explain what you are modelling instead.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{A real-data example}

The \fn{epil} data records seizure counts for epilepsy patients. Each patient
contributes a baseline count over 8 weeks and four follow-up counts over 2 weeks
each. The exposure differs between those periods, so the counts are not
comparable without an offset.

<<epil-offset>>=
data(epil, package = "MASS")
head(epil[, c("y", "trt", "base", "age", "period", "subject")], 5)

## Model follow-up seizures per 2-week period, adjusting for the 8-week baseline
epil$lbase <- log(epil$base / 4)          # baseline rescaled to a 2-week rate
epil$lage  <- log(epil$age)
me <- glm(y ~ lbase + trt + lage, family = poisson, data = epil)
round(exp(cbind(IRR = coef(me), confint.default(me))), 3)
@

The treatment IRR of \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(me))["trtprogabide"], 3)} suggests a
small reduction in seizure rate on progabide. But these are repeated measures on
the same patients, so these standard errors are not trustworthy --- we return to
this dataset properly in Chapter 15.

\begin{warningbox}[Offsets do not fix clustering]
An offset handles \emph{unequal opportunity}. It does nothing about
\emph{non-independence}. If the same patient appears five times in your data, you
still need a random effect or a robust standard error on top of the offset.
\end{warningbox}
